---
import Article from "../components/article.astro";
import { Definition } from "../components/definition";
import Heading from "../components/heading.astro";
import Paragraph from "../components/paragraph.astro";
import SectionLayout from "../layouts/section.astro";
---

<SectionLayout
  title="Allomorphy"
  position="4"
  previousPath="/inflection-and-derivation"
  nextPath="/suppletion-and-lexemes"
>
  <Article>
    <Heading>Allomorphy</Heading>

    <Paragraph>
      You’re familiar with the English prefix “in-”, which generally means “not”
      or “opposite of”. Can we use this same prefix for all words where we need
      a negative counterpart?
    </Paragraph>

    <!-- TODO: Add the interactive negative-prefix allomorphy demo. -->

    <Paragraph>
      Wait a minute, you used “im-” for impossible and “il-” for illegal! The
      prefix changed depending on its surrounding context. This phenomenon is
      known as <b>allomorphy</b>.
    </Paragraph>

    <Definition
      term="allomorph"
      category="noun"
      ipa="ˈaləmɔːrf"
      definition="any of the different variant forms of a single morpheme"
    />

    <Paragraph>
      Allomorphy occurs when a single morpheme has multiple
      <b>phonological variants</b>—called allomorphs—depending on the linguistic
      context surrounding it.
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph>
      Why <i>phonological</i>? Because linguistics prioritizes the spoken form
      of language over its written realizations. Hence, there may even be
      allomorphs of a morpheme that are <i>spelt the same</i> but <i
        >sound different</i
      >.
    </Paragraph>

    <!-- TODO: Add the interactive plural-sound allomorph selection demo. -->

    <Paragraph>
      The English plural suffix is the perfect example of a morpheme with
      multiple allomorphs, <i>including</i> allomorphs with the same spelling but
      different pronunciations.
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph>
      Awesome stuff. You’ll now see the different allomorphs of any morpheme
      that you locate in the English toolkit, and you’ll also be able to create
      allomorphs in your conlang through the conlang panel!
    </Paragraph>
  </Article>
</SectionLayout>
